    sections: [
      {
        type: 'about',
        tabLabel: 'About',
        pages: [
          {
            title: 'Tàihé A Bāo 泰和A包',
            subtitle: 'Card Game',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Tàihé A Bāo (泰和A包) is a Chinese climbing game from Taihe county, Jiangxi province. Four players: one declarer vs the other three each hand. 40-card deck (remove 2s, 3s, 8s); rank A, K, Q, J, 10, 9, 7, 6, 5, 4. Cards drawn from deck counterclockwise (not dealt). Bidding: declare or pass; if all pass, second round—call for a card (exchange); last caller is declarer. Play: declarer leads combinations; others beat or pass. Combinations: singles, pairs, sequences, double sequences, three-of-a-kind, four-of-a-kind (bombs beat any non-bomb). First out wins; if declarer wins they collect stakes from each opponent. Often played for money.',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'Chinese climbing game from Taihe (泰和) county, Jiangxi (江西) province. A correspondent learned it there around 2013. Name refers to the Ace (A) as highest card. Part of the broader Chinese climbing/shedding family.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Setup',
            subtitle: 'Table, deck, equipment, and deal',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '4 (3 or 5 in variants)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '40 cards (remove 2s, 3s, 8s from 52)',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: '52-card deck (40 used), optional stakes',
              },
              {
                type: 'paragraph',
                text: 'The deck is shuffled and placed face-down in the center. Each player receives 10 cards by taking one card at a time in turn in counterclockwise order.',
              },
            ],
          },
          {
            title: 'Variations',
            subtitle: 'Known ways this game changes',
            content: [
              {
                type: 'list',
                style: 'unordered',
                items: [
                  {
                    text: 'Five players: Five players; each receives eight cards. A hand declared in the first round of bidding is worth six stakes against each opponent.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Three players: Remove the Ace of Spades; 39 cards. Each player receives 13 cards. A set of three Aces counts as four Aces (unbeatable). First-round declare worth four stakes per opponent.',
                  },
                  {
                    text: 'Without Aces: Kings are highest. Kings may be exchanged in bidding and used in sequences. Five players: also remove King of Spades, seven cards each. Three players: 12 cards each, deck otherwise unchanged.',
                  },
                ],
              },
            ],
          },
        ],
      },
    ],
